<!--
    A Swift string object printed as another literal's text.  swiftc passes a
    String literal of 16 bytes or more as two words: its count with flags, and
    the object word (literal - 0x20) | 1<<63.  When the tag is ORed in after a
    join, the untagged object reaches ActionConstantPtr as a bare constant.
    Large literals are 16-byte aligned, so it is the start of, or a point 16
    bytes into, the literal stored before, and the action's char-array arms
    printed that literal's text in place of the address.

    pick and level are swiftc -O output (Swift 6.4.2, arm64 Mach-O) for

        func pick(_ flag: Bool) -> String {
            flag ? "chosen when the flag is set (long)"
                 : "chosen when the flag is clear (long)" }
        func level(_ n: Int) -> String {
            switch n { case 0: return "level zero (short-ish)"
                       case 1: return "level one (short-ish)"
                       default: return "level two (short-ish)" } }

    with the __cstring section they read.  Before the fix pick's set path
    printed "flag is clear (long)" (16 bytes into the clear literal, the
    interior arm), and level printed "level one (short-ish)" for case 0 and
    "level two (short-ish)" for case 1 (the exact-hit arm) and "flag is set
    (long)" for the default.  `map addr` stands in for the strings analysis
    pass, which does not run on a bytechunk image.

    ctl_join is hand-written: pick's values with the bias applied after the
    join, the shape swiftc emits for a large rawValue switch.  Its literal
    addresses meet an INT_ADD before the tag, so they keep their text.

    Stage: P9 emission (ActionConstantPtr, kuna_swiftstrobj), strict fix, no
    option.  #1-#6 fail before the fix; #7-#10 pass with and without it.
-->
